        {
          "type": "compare",
          "say": "Who starts each assignment? In a push model, the scheduler sends tasks to workers. It has the global view, so it can pack tasks tightly, but it must track every worker's load precisely. In a pull model, idle workers ask for work. They only take what they can run, which gives natural backpressure, and scales more simply. That is why pull is often preferred.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Push or <em>pull</em>?",
            "left": {
              "at": "push",
              "label": "Push",
              "title": "Scheduler sends",
              "body": "Global view allows tight bin-packing, but it must track every worker's load."
            },
            "right": {
              "at": "pull",
              "label": "Pull",
              "title": "Workers ask",
              "tone": "good",
              "body": "Workers take only what they can run: natural backpressure, simpler to scale."
            },
            "verdict": {
              "at": "preferred",
              "text": "Pull is the usual default; push when placement matters.",
              "code": "worker.poll()"
            }
          }
        }
      ]
